Does the Perl world need another object-oriented programming framework? To be honest, probably not. But here’s why you might want to give Marlin a try anyway. Most of your constructors and accessors will be implemented in XS and be really, really fast.
If you accept a few basic principles like “attributes should usually be read-only”, it can be really, really concise to declare a class and its attributes.
An example
use v5.20.0; use experimental qw(signatures);
# Import useful constants, types, etc. use Marlin::Util -all, -lexical; use Types::Common -all, -lexical;
package Person { use Marlin 'given_name!' => NonEmptyStr, 'family_name!' => NonEmptyStr, 'name_style' => { enum => [qw/western eastern/], default => 'western' }, …
